// Families 7 and 8 of the catalogue (docs/ridonculous-race-scenes.md §7, §8): information, deception
// and rumour (lie, share-info, overheard, secret-used, rumour, spy, bluff), and sabotage and its
// aftermath (push, ram, steal, kidnap-pet, course-sabotage, fake-shortcut, mind-game, admin-sabotage,
// free-ride, revenge, comeuppance, save-your-enemy). Written to docs/rr-writers-brief.md.
// Who is who: a acted (the liar, the saboteur, the one who helps), b is a's partner, c the one on
// the other team a deals with, d c's partner. Only villains and schemers lie or sabotage (the
// engine refuses anyone else), so b is often the uneasy one.
// The knowledge rule (js/rr/scenes/sabotage.js): in a sabotage's `works` and `backfires` endings
// nobody on the other team saw it, so c and d only ever speak there as confused bystanders (`opt`)
// and never name it as sabotage; in `seen` they or a crew member saw it, and the penalty (an hour)
// is served later at the mat, so the scene only fears it.
// What the engine does not keep, and how the lines live with it:
//   - what was lied about, stolen, kidnapped or broken is not recorded, so each entry sets up its
//     own object in a beat (a tip, a map, a lucky cap) and names it from there;
//   - revenge fires on the last sabotage done to a's team even when nobody saw it, so a general
//     entry says what it cost ("you set us back on purpose"), and only sabKind-gated ones name it;
//   - rumour fires when anybody has been Boomeranged, not only a's team, so nobody says "on us".
